<Project>

  <!-- Build output must stay inside the repository. Enforced, not documented, because the
       documented version did not hold: `-p:BaseOutputPath=<%TEMP%\...>` was copied into a dozen
       older plans under docs/plans/ as the workaround for MSB3027 (the running app locking bin\),
       and it costs twice over:
         1. RepoPaths.SolutionRoot() (tests/LocalScribe.App.Tests/XamlHygieneTests.cs) walks UP from
            the test assembly looking for .git. Relocated outside the repo that walk finds nothing,
            so every RepoPaths-anchored test - the source-text pins this whole Tier 1A round relies
            on - either fails or silently validates a different tree.
         2. OBSERVED on this machine 2026-08-05: 27 build folders totalling ~500 MB accumulated at
            the ROOT of C:, because a mistyped or short-form (8.3) path resolves somewhere nobody
            looks. Silent, and it repeats on every run.
       The correct answer to MSB3027 is to close the app, never to relocate the output.

       Deliberately NO opt-out property: an escape hatch is how the rule got defeated the first
       time. A build that genuinely needs a different output location changes this file, in a
       commit, with a reason.

       F8 (final whole-branch review, 2026-08-05): this target used to live INSIDE
       src/Directory.Build.props, so MSBuild - which walks UP from each project and stops at the
       first Directory.Build.props - never reached it from tests\. The common routes were caught
       transitively (a test project's src\ ProjectReferences inherit the global property and error),
       but `dotnet build tests/LocalScribe.App.Tests -o C:\temp\x` with the "no-dependencies" flag
       evaluates no src\ project at all: no guard ran, the test assembly landed outside the repo, and
       RepoPaths.SolutionRoot() walked past into whatever .git it found first - the exact "silently
       validates a different tree" failure this guard was built for, in reason #1 of its own
       rationale. So the target lives HERE, in ONE copy, imported by BOTH src/Directory.Build.props
       and tests/Directory.Build.props. Two copies could drift; one cannot.

       Location matters: $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory) is THIS file's directory regardless of which
       props file imported it, so '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..' resolves to the repository root
       from build\ exactly as it did from src\. Moving this file changes that - keep it one level
       below the repo root.

       Deliberately a .targets file under build\ rather than a repo-root Directory.Build.props:
       a root props would silently apply to all 13 csproj files, including tools/generate-icon and
       tools/UiaProbe, and would drag the <Version> stamp with it - which is why
       BuildVersionTests.The_props_file_is_scoped_to_src_and_not_the_repo_root asserts no such file
       exists. tests\ imports the guard and NOTHING else: test assemblies must not carry the
       product's 0.9.0 version stamp or the git-sha InformationalVersion.

       MEASURED 2026-08-05 (SDK 10.0.302), which is why both properties are checked and PublishDir
       is not:
         dotnet build/test -p:BaseOutputPath=X -> BaseOutputPath=X, OutDir=X\<Config>\<TFM>\
         dotnet build -o X                     -> BaseOutputPath=bin\ (!), OutDir=OutputPath=X
         dotnet publish -o X                   -> PublishDir=X only; BaseOutputPath and OutDir stay
                                                  inside the repo.
       So OutDir catches both escape routes and the helper-publish workflow
       (`dotnet publish src/LocalScribe.Assistant ... -o <scratch>`, docs/plans/2026-07-19-*) is
       untouched. BaseOutputPath is checked separately only to name the offending flag in the error.

       PrepareForBuild is the hook because its MakeDir task is what first creates $(OutDir) - firing
       before it means a rejected build writes nothing outside the repo. VERIFIED 2026-08-05: after
       a rejected run the target directory does not exist. -->
  <Target Name="EnsureBuildOutputStaysInsideRepo" BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <!-- NormalizeDirectory combines then GetFullPath's, so it handles a relative value, an
           absolute value and a ..\ escape identically, and guarantees the trailing slash the
           StartsWith below depends on (without it, a sibling F:\LocalScribeOther\bin would be
           accepted as "inside" F:\LocalScribe). Lowercased because Windows paths are
           case-insensitive and MSBuild's StartsWith is not. -->
      <_LsRepoRoot>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..'))</_LsRepoRoot>
      <_LsRepoRootKey>$(_LsRepoRoot.ToLowerInvariant())</_LsRepoRootKey>
      <_LsBaseOutputFull>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)', '$(BaseOutputPath)'))</_LsBaseOutputFull>
      <_LsOutDirFull>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)', '$(OutDir)'))</_LsOutDirFull>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Error Condition="!$(_LsBaseOutputFull.ToLowerInvariant().StartsWith('$(_LsRepoRootKey)'))"
           Code="LS0001"
           Text="BaseOutputPath resolves to '$(_LsBaseOutputFull)', which is OUTSIDE the repository at '$(_LsRepoRoot)'. Building outside the repo breaks RepoPaths.SolutionRoot() (it walks up for .git, so RepoPaths-anchored tests fail or validate the wrong tree) and has been observed leaving stray build folders on a drive root. Drop the -p:BaseOutputPath flag; if the running app is locking bin\ (MSB3027), close the app instead." />
    <Error Condition="!$(_LsOutDirFull.ToLowerInvariant().StartsWith('$(_LsRepoRootKey)'))"
           Code="LS0002"
           Text="OutDir resolves to '$(_LsOutDirFull)', which is OUTSIDE the repository at '$(_LsRepoRoot)'. Use 'dotnet publish -o &lt;dir&gt;' (which sets PublishDir only) if you need output elsewhere; 'dotnet build -o &lt;dir&gt;' relocates the build itself and breaks RepoPaths.SolutionRoot()." />
  </Target>

</Project>
